        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(zalouser): settle waitForExit when abort signal already fired",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where stopping a ZaloUser gateway account during or immediately after connection startup could leave channel shutdown waiting forever. This happened when the gateway stop signal was already aborted before the monitor registered its listener, or became aborted while startup allowlist/group resolution was in progress.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`AbortSignal` does not replay an `abort` event to listeners registered after the signal has fired. The monitor now owns its shutdown lifecycle before asynchronous startup work, handles an already-aborted signal synchronously, and rechecks the signal after startup resolution before creating the Zalo listener.\n\nThe fix stays inside the ZaloUser plugin and adds no config, protocol, SDK, or dependency surface.\n\n## User Impact\n\nZaloUser stop and restart operations now settle promptly instead of hanging during fast gateway restarts or overlapping account lifecycle transitions. An aborted startup also avoids restoring a Zalo listener session.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe committed tests exercise the real `monitorZalouserProvider` function with only the Zalo API/listener boundary mocked:\n\n- A pre-aborted signal returns immediately and never calls `startZaloListener`.\n- A signal aborted while the friends lookup Promise is pending completes startup resolution but never calls `startZaloListener`.\n- Existing mutable allowlist and group-name startup resolution remains covered.\n\nFocused validation on head `a4be8e9450`:\n\n```text\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\nTests       25 passed (25)\n```\n\nCommand:\n\n```text\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/zalouser/src/monitor.group-gating.test.ts extensions/zalouser/src/monitor.account-scope.test.ts\n```\n\nThe platform contract is also directly observable in Node: adding an `abort` listener after `AbortController.abort()` does not invoke that listener, while checking `signal.aborted` catches the state synchronously.\n\nA configured ZaloUser account is not available in this environment, so the evidence stops at the production monitor with the external Zalo boundary mocked. Maintainers may either run the same stop/restart scenario with a configured account or explicitly accept this focused boundary proof.\n",
